  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 202 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nBroad upper-level ridging is spreading east into the middle of the \ncountry. In the lower-levels, southerly to southwesterly level winds \nwill prevail and this combination will lead to temperatures\nclimbing to well above normal values, which are now in the mid \n80s. Highs on Sunday will climb into the middle to upper 90s for\nmost locations, with nearly even chances for 100 degree \ntemperatures in our far southern and southwestern zones. While \nit will be very hot, the combination of temperatures and \nhumidity will keep most areas from seeing heat index values \nclimbing near or exceeding 105. So don't anticipate the need for\na heat advisory at this time. 500mb heights increase into \nMonday with the greatest heights just south of the area across \nArkansas and into southern Missouri. This will support even \nwarmer temperatures and a greater probability of more areas \nseeing 100+ degree temperatures, including the Kansas City area.\nPersistent southerly flow should lead to an uptick in moisture \nsuch that when combined with the well above normal temperatures,\nthe area is more likely to see heat index values near or \nexceeding 105. This trend continues through late next week and \nanticipate some kind of heat headline needed for at least a \nportion of the forecast area. Right now this looks more \nprobable in southeastern zones and potentially areas along and \nsouth of the Missouri River. \n\nSwitching to precipitation potential, chances look very low to non-\nexistent for the next week. However, for tomorrow morning, a similar \nsetup to this morning will play out. The main focus for \nprecipitation will be along the nose of the low-level jet, generally \nnorth of the forecast area. However, there is still some mid-level \nascent noted moving into northern/northwestern Missouri. And also \nsome potential for an outflow boundary to move into the area, aiding \nany subtle broader-scale ascent. Have added PoPs generally around \n20% for tomorrow morning to account for this lower confidence/lower \nprobability potential. For the remainder of the forecast, there is \nlimited potential overall. But on Wednesday night into Thursday, as \nthe upper ridging weakens slightly, and a weak boundary moves into \nthe area, that some slight chances move into northern Missouri. \nRight now, chances look less than 20% so more likely to remain dry \nthan to see any precipitation.",
